One architectural change I'd make


Don't make Q ordinary world data.


E, R, S, T, P, and Γ describe the world and NEXUS's knowledge of it.


Q describes what the user is currently asking NEXUS to project.


So conceptually:


WORLD STATE
(E, R, S, T, P, Γ)
          │
          ↓
     QUERY CONTEXT Q
          │
          ↓
   PROJECTION ENGINE
          │
     ┌────┼────┐
     ↓    ↓    ↓
   WebGL API Terminal Report



That preserves your original renderer-independence principle.


Phase 0 recommendation


Don't attempt to ingest all of this immediately.


Build the vertical slice around roughly:


San Francisco → ~100 entities → 10–12 entity classes → 8–10 relationship types → bitemporal state → provenance → cryptographic hashes → confidence vector → query context.


For the new domains, I'd specifically implement:




One ecosystem


Several species/populations


A few legal instruments


A small intellectual-property representation


A network topology example


Content hashes + digital signatures


One ledger-anchor representation


Viewport/H3/LOD query context




That gives you something much more important than a huge static ontology: a demonstrable end-to-end chain from real-world entity → relationship → state → evidence → cryptographic verification → temporal history → spatial aggregation → user projection.


And that is the point at which WORLD // NEXUS starts behaving like a genuine world-state engine, rather than simply being a very large database schema.

